# The `char` arm of the element-wise arithmetic table (src/tychoc.c:1091): `+`
# and `-` are defined element-wise on a `[char]`, and `* / %` are not.
#
# This is the least directly testable rule in the resolver, because `char` has no
# type keyword (docs/spec/03-types.md:75) so no annotation can name `[char]`.
# It is reachable anyway through INFERENCE: `char_at(s, i)` (src/tychoc.c:4689)
# yields a `char`, so an array literal of `char_at` calls infers `[char]` without
# the type ever being written. That is the whole trick this fixture exists to
# record -- before it, the only witnesses for the narrow rule were the SCALAR
# rejects (tests/reject/char_int_mul.ty and friends), which exercise the char
# arm at src/tychoc.c:6432-6434, not the element-wise one.
#
# The refusing half is tests/reject/char_elem_mul.ty, char_elem_div.ty and
# char_elem_mod.ty -- one per operator the rule excludes.
fn main():
    lo := "abc"
    sp := "   "
    a  := [char_at(lo, 0), char_at(lo, 1), char_at(lo, 2)]   # ['a','b','c']
    sh := [char_at(sp, 0), char_at(sp, 1), char_at(sp, 2)]   # three 0x20 bytes

    # element-wise `-` on [char]: subtract the case bit, three chars at a time
    up := a - sh
    println(str(up[0] == 'A') + "," + str(up[1] == 'B') + "," + str(up[2] == 'C'))

    # element-wise `+` on [char]: and back again
    back := up + sh
    println(str(back[0] == 'a') + "," + str(back[1] == 'b') + "," + str(back[2] == 'c'))

    # the result really is a [char], not a widened [int]: it appends to a string
    # as a glyph (docs/spec/09-expressions.md:36), which only a char does
    s := ""
    for i := 0; i < 3; i += 1:
        s = s + up[i]
    println(s)
